# Lifecycle communications

Permanent rule for all customer communication: transactional emails, lifecycle emails, in-app notifications, and product messaging. Complements `voice-and-boundaries.mdc` and `draper-honeycopy.mdc` (voice), `trust-and-claims.mdc` (honest content), and `security-and-privacy.mdc` (no secrets in messages).

> A polished lifecycle system should feel like the product is paying attention, not like a marketing sequence is chasing the user.

> A premium SaaS experience is defined as much by what happens when something goes wrong as by what happens when everything works.

## Communication categories

Distinguish and never blur: transactional, security, billing, product notification, onboarding, educational lifecycle, marketing, operational, and support communication. Category determines consent, suppression, urgency, and channel.

## Every communication must have

- A legitimate trigger tied to real system state.
- A clear purpose.
- A defined audience.
- A single dominant action.
- Accurate product state at send time.
- Accurate branding.
- Appropriate urgency.
- A fallback destination.
- An ownership and support path.
- A suppression policy.
- A retry policy where applicable.
- An analytics purpose where appropriate.

## Consent and suppression

Apply consent and preference behavior appropriate to the category. Security, account, and essential billing communications must not be suppressed as ordinary marketing. Marketing and educational lifecycle messages honor opt-out. Never send a transactional message dressed as marketing to bypass preferences, and never withhold a security alert because a user unsubscribed from a newsletter.

## Delivery and craft requirements

- Branded sender identity with consistent sender names.
- Appropriate reply handling (monitored reply-to or a clear no-reply with a real support path).
- Verified sending domains (SPF, DKIM, DMARC configured before launch).
- Professional plain-text alternatives.
- Accessible templates and mobile-safe layouts.
- Useful preview text.
- Clear links and a clear support contact (`company.mdc` identity).

## Hard prohibitions

- No fake urgency.
- No invented activity or fabricated engagement.
- No misleading personalization.
- No sensitive information in subject lines.
- No passwords, secrets, or complete API credentials in messages.
- No customer data leakage across recipients (verify recipient scoping).
- No duplicate sends from retried jobs (idempotent send keys).
- No message claiming success before the underlying operation actually succeeded.

## Voice

Product and email language share the approved Fajita voice (`draper-honeycopy.mdc`) without sacrificing clarity. Confident, specific, calm. No em dashes. No AI slop.

## Notifications versus email

Product notifications and emails must not repeat the same information unnecessarily. Decide the right channel per event in `notification-policy.md`. All communications must reflect real system state at delivery time.

## Repository-specific baseline (known facts)

- No email/sending provider is wired yet. Resend is available as an MCP integration but is not a project dependency and has no configured key. Sender domain, provider, and templates are `[UNRESOLVED]`.
- DataFast is the analytics layer; communication analytics should reuse defined goals where meaningful without leaking PII.
- Company identity for sender and footers: Fajita, 1001 S Main St, Ste 600, Kalispell, MT 59901 (`company.mdc`).

## Required memory updates

- `.cursor/maturity-memory/communication-map.md`
- `.cursor/maturity-memory/notification-policy.md`

Before implementing welcome emails, auth emails, security alerts, onboarding emails, activation nudges, processing notifications, digests, usage alerts, billing emails, failed-payment emails, upgrade confirmations, cancellation emails, re-engagement, in-app notification centers, or support communications, invoke `lifecycle-communication-director`.

## Avoid overengineering

Use a managed sending provider rather than custom email infrastructure. Start with the essential transactional and security messages; add lifecycle and marketing sequences only when a real journey needs them. Do not build a notification center before there is something worth notifying users about.

